# Psychedelics. No amounts, preparation, sourcing, or mixing tips.
from data_new import X,t,CW
S=["Nichols DE. Psychedelics. Pharmacol Rev 2016"]
X("lsd","LSD (lysergic acid diethylamide)","psy","Semi-synthetic ergoline psychedelic; potent 5-HT2A agonist.","Acid, tabs, blotter, L, Lucy; Delysid (former Sandoz brand).",
"Albert Hofmann synthesized LSD at Sandoz in 1938 and discovered its effects by accident on April 16, 1943, taking it deliberately three days later ('Bicycle Day'). Sandoz supplied it as Delysid for psychiatric research. The CIA tested it in MKUltra. Recreational use exploded in the 1960s counterculture, leading to bans in 1966-1970.",
"Used in psychiatric research in the 1950s and 1960s; now in clinical trials for anxiety (an LSD formulation, MM120, received FDA breakthrough designation for anxiety in 2024).","Spread through the 1960s counterculture, music and festival scenes, and remains common.","US Schedule I; UN 1971 Convention Schedule I.",
["LSD's character is long, clear and 'electric': vivid colors, patterns breathing on surfaces, deep emotional and philosophical thinking, and a stimulating energy.","Time stretches over a very long experience; thoughts can loop, and people may confront difficult memories ('bad trip'), which is the main danger alongside risky behavior.","LSD has very low physical toxicity, but it can trigger lasting psychosis in vulnerable people, and blotters sold as LSD sometimes contain NBOMes, which are far more dangerous."],
["Effects begin 30 to 90 minutes after taking it: restlessness, yawning, and a shift in how things look.","Over the next hour colors brighten and patterns appear; emotions intensify.","The peak, about 3 to 5 hours in, brings the strongest visual and mental changes and loss of the usual sense of time.","Effects remain strong for several hours.","From about 8 hours, effects taper slowly over several more hours.","Difficulty sleeping and a reflective afterglow are common.","Tiredness the next day; tolerance lasts several days."],
{"oral":t("30-90 min","1-2 h","3-5 h","5-8 h","8-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Blotter paper, liquid, or gel tabs held in the mouth and swallowed."},
"There is no known human death from LSD toxicity alone at typical levels; deaths result from accidents, suicide, or substances sold as LSD (especially NBOMes).",
"Blotter paper squares, often printed with art; sometimes liquid or small gelatin squares. A bitter or numbing taste suggests an NBOME, not LSD."+CW,
"LSD binds 5-HT2A receptors extremely tightly; a lid-like part of the receptor closes over it, which helps explain its long duration.",
ext=("LSD is absorbed from the mouth and gut; very small amounts are active, which is why blotters can carry it.","Pupils dilate and heart rate and temperature rise slightly; some people feel nauseated or cold.","At the peak, 5-HT2A activation in the cortex and thalamus loosens the brain's normal filtering, producing hallucination-like visual effects and altered sense of self.","Physical toxicity is very low, but panic, confusion and accidents are real risks, and people with personal or family history of psychosis or bipolar disorder are at higher risk of lasting harm.","LSD's half-life is about 3 to 4 hours, but its receptor binding keeps effects going far longer.","A minority report hallucinogen persisting perception disorder (HPPD): lasting visual trails or static."),
src=S+["Hofmann A. LSD: My Problem Child (1980)"])
X("lsd-analogs","LSD analogs (1P-LSD, ALD-52, AL-LAD, 1cP-LSD, ETH-LAD)","psy","Lysergamides closely related to LSD.","1P-LSD, 1cP-LSD, 1V-LSD, ALD-52, AL-LAD, ETH-LAD, LSZ.",
"Several were studied by Hofmann and later by Alexander Shulgin; from 2015 1P-LSD and others were sold online as legal research chemicals in Europe. Many are now controlled.","None.","Sold online as 'legal LSD'.","Varies; 1P-LSD and others are controlled in many EU countries; in the US, analog laws may apply, and ALD-52 is Schedule I.",
["Many analogs (1P-LSD, ALD-52) are thought to act as prodrugs that turn into LSD in the body, so effects feel very similar.","AL-LAD is often described as shorter and more visual; ETH-LAD as stronger.","Because they are sold online with uncertain purity, contents may not match the label."],
["Onset in 30 to 90 minutes.","Visual and emotional changes build.","Peak at 2 to 5 hours.","Plateau several hours.","Taper over hours.","Insomnia.","Next-day tiredness."],
{"oral":t("30-90 min","1-2 h","2-5 h","4-7 h","7-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Blotter paper."},"Risks mirror LSD; physical toxicity appears low, but contents are uncertain.","Blotter squares."+CW,"1-acyl analogs are hydrolyzed to LSD in the body.",
ext=("Most 1-acyl analogs are converted to LSD by enzymes in the blood and liver.","Body signs mirror LSD.","At peak, effects are those of LSD or a close relative at 5-HT2A.","Panic, confusion and accidents are the main dangers.","Durations are similar to or slightly shorter than LSD.","HPPD and lasting anxiety are possible."),src=S+["Brandt SD. et al. Return of the lysergamides. Drug Test Anal 2016-2020"])
X("psilocybin","Psilocybin mushrooms","psy","Tryptamine prodrug converted to psilocin, a 5-HT2A agonist.","Magic mushrooms, shrooms, Psilocybe cubensis and other species; synthetic psilocybin (COMP360) in trials.",
"Mushrooms have been used in Mesoamerican rituals for centuries. R. Gordon Wasson's 1957 Life magazine article introduced them to the West; Albert Hofmann isolated psilocybin in 1958. Oregon (2020) and Colorado (2022) legalized supervised use.",
"Clinical trials for depression, end-of-life anxiety and addiction; FDA gave breakthrough designation for treatment-resistant depression (2018) and major depression (2019).","Widely used recreationally and in religious or 'microdosing' contexts.","US Schedule I (Oregon and Colorado allow licensed use); UN 1971 Schedule I (the mushrooms themselves are not explicitly listed).",
["Psilocybin feels earthier and more emotional than LSD, often described as warm, introspective and 'organic', with flowing visuals.","The experience is shorter than LSD and more body-centered, with nausea at first.","The main dangers are psychological: overwhelming fear, confusion and accidents. Poisonous look-alike mushrooms (such as Galerina or Amanita species) can be deadly."],
["Effects begin 20 to 60 minutes after eating: yawning, nausea and a heaviness.","Visuals and emotional shifts build.","The peak at about 1.5 to 3 hours brings the strongest perceptual and emotional effects.","Plateau about 1 to 2 hours.","Taper over 2 hours.","Reflective afterglow, tiredness.","Usually little next-day effect."],
{"oral":t("20-60 min","1 h","1.5-3 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-8 h","next day")},{"oral":"Eaten dried or fresh, or as tea or chocolates."},
"Physical toxicity is low; risks are panic, accidents, and poisoning from misidentified mushrooms.","Dried mushrooms with thin stems and brown caps; they may bruise blue. Never eat wild mushrooms without expert identification."+CW,
"Psilocybin is dephosphorylated into psilocin, which carries the effect.",
ext=("Psilocybin is converted in the gut and liver to psilocin, which crosses into the brain.","Nausea and yawning are common early as psilocin acts on gut serotonin receptors.","At the peak, 5-HT2A activation reduces activity in the brain's default mode network, linked to loss of the usual sense of self.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise modestly; the main risks are psychological.","Psilocin's half-life is about 2 to 3 hours.","Clinical studies report lasting mood benefits in some participants, but also rare lasting anxiety or HPPD."),src=S+["Wasson RG. Seeking the magic mushroom. Life 1957","Carhart-Harris RL. et al. NEJM 2021"])
X("dmt","DMT (N,N-dimethyltryptamine)","psy","Short-acting tryptamine psychedelic found in many plants.","DMT, dimitri, the spirit molecule; changa (smoking blend).",
"DMT was first synthesized in 1931 by Richard Manske; Stephen Szára discovered its effects in 1956. Rick Strassman's 1990s studies described 'entity' encounters.","No approved use; studies of IV DMT for depression are ongoing.","Vaped or smoked for brief, intense 'breakthrough' experiences.","US Schedule I; UN 1971 Schedule I.",
["Inhaled DMT is a sudden, overwhelming experience: within seconds, geometric visions and a feeling of leaving one's body, often with apparent encounters with 'beings'.","It is very short, often over within 15 to 20 minutes, which makes it seem less dangerous than it is.","Heart rate and blood pressure spike, and people can fall or drop lit pipes."],
["Effects begin within seconds of inhaling.","A rapid rush of sound and visuals.","Peak at 2 to 5 minutes, with loss of contact with surroundings.","Plateau a few minutes.","Return over 10 to 20 minutes.","Afterglow.","Usually none."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","30 s-1 min","2-5 min","5-10 min","10-20 min","under 1 h","none")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Vaporized or smoked."},
"Physical toxicity is low, but blood pressure spikes and falls are risks, especially with heart disease.","DMT is a white to orange-yellow crystalline powder."+CW,"DMT is broken down very quickly by monoamine oxidase (MAO).",
ext=("Inhaled DMT reaches the brain in seconds; swallowed DMT is destroyed by gut MAO and is inactive unless combined with an MAO inhibitor (as in ayahuasca).","Heart rate and blood pressure rise sharply in the first minutes.","At peak, DMT activates 5-HT2A and sigma-1 receptors; EEG studies show the brain shifting to a dreamlike state.","People with heart disease or high blood pressure face the greatest risk.","MAO breaks DMT down within minutes, ending effects rapidly.","Afterwards, people may feel shaken; integration of intense experiences can take time."),src=S+["Strassman R. DMT: The Spirit Molecule (2000)","Timmermann C. et al. Sci Rep 2019"])
X("ayahuasca","Ayahuasca","psy","Amazonian brew combining DMT-containing plants with MAO-inhibiting Banisteriopsis caapi vine.","Ayahuasca, yagé, hoasca, la medicina; 'pharmahuasca' (pharmaceutical analogues).",
"Used for centuries by Indigenous peoples of the Amazon basin. Brazilian churches (Santo Daime, União do Vegetal) use it as a sacrament; in 2006 the US Supreme Court protected UDV's religious use (Gonzales v. O Centro Espírita). 'Ayahuasca tourism' grew in Peru from the 2000s.",
"No approved use; studied for depression and addiction.","Taken in ceremonial retreats, increasingly by tourists.","DMT is US Schedule I; specific churches have religious exemptions. Legal or tolerated in Peru, Brazil and some other countries.",
["Ayahuasca's character is long, emotional and visionary: vivid inner imagery, strong emotional release, and often vomiting, which participants call 'purging'.","The MAO inhibitors in the vine make swallowed DMT active and also block breakdown of other substances, so it can cause dangerous interactions with antidepressants, stimulants, and some foods.","Deaths at retreats have involved heart problems, drug interactions, other plants added to the brew (such as toé/brugmansia), and violence or neglect."],
["Effects begin 30 to 60 minutes after drinking, with nausea.","Visions and emotional waves build; vomiting is common.","The peak at 1.5 to 2.5 hours brings intense imagery.","Plateau about 1 to 2 hours.","Taper over 1 to 2 hours.","Tiredness and introspection.","Next-day fatigue."],
{"oral":t("30-60 min","1 h","1.5-2.5 h","2-3 h","3-6 h","6-12 h","next day")},{"oral":"Drunk as a brew."},
"Serotonin syndrome with SSRIs or other drugs, high blood pressure, and dehydration from vomiting are the main dangers.","A thick brown liquid with a bitter taste.","Harmine and harmaline from the vine inhibit MAO-A, letting DMT survive digestion.",
ext=("Harmala alkaloids inhibit MAO-A in the gut and liver within minutes, allowing DMT to reach the bloodstream.","Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common as both serotonin and harmala alkaloids act on the gut.","At peak, DMT's 5-HT2A effects combine with harmaline's own effects, producing long visionary states.","MAO inhibition can cause serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, SNRIs, MDMA, tramadol, DXM and others, and hypertensive crisis with stimulants or tyramine-rich foods.","Effects fade as DMT is cleared, but MAO inhibition lasts longer.","People on antidepressants are at the highest risk; medical advice is essential."),src=S+["Gonzales v. O Centro Espírita Beneficente União do Vegetal, 546 U.S. 418 (2006)"])
X("5-meo-dmt","5-MeO-DMT","psy","Very potent, short-acting tryptamine; found in some plants and the venom gland secretion of the Sonoran Desert toad.","5-MeO, 'toad', Bufo alvarius (Incilius alvarius) secretion; GH001 (in trials).",
"First synthesized in 1936 and identified in plants in 1959. Use of toad secretion was popularized in the 1980s and rose in the 2010s through retreats; wild toads have suffered from collection.","No approved use; GH001 is in trials for depression.","Used in retreats for intense 'non-dual' experiences.","US Schedule I (2011).",
["5-MeO-DMT is less visual than DMT: an overwhelming dissolution of self, sometimes described as white light or 'nothingness'.","People may become unresponsive, thrash, or vomit; choking and falls are dangers.","Deaths have been reported at ceremonies, and combining with MAOIs is especially dangerous."],
["Effects begin within seconds of inhaling.","Rapid loss of contact with surroundings.","Peak within minutes.","Short plateau.","Return over 15 to 30 minutes.","Emotional afterglow or distress.","Usually none; some report lingering anxiety."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","under 1 min","1-5 min","5-15 min","15-30 min","hours","none")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Vaporized."},
"Serious risks include breathing problems, seizures, choking and serotonin syndrome with MAOIs.","Synthetic 5-MeO-DMT is a white powder; toad secretion dries to a crystalline film. Toad secretion also contains bufotoxins, which affect the heart."+CW,"5-MeO-DMT acts strongly at 5-HT1A as well as 5-HT2A.",
ext=("Inhaled 5-MeO-DMT reaches the brain within seconds.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise; muscle movements may be involuntary.","At peak, strong 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A activation produces loss of awareness; people may stop responding entirely.","Combined with MAO inhibitors (including ayahuasca), 5-MeO-DMT has caused fatal serotonin toxicity.","Rapid breakdown by MAO ends effects in under an hour.","Some people experience 'reactivations' or anxiety for days after."),src=S+["Ermakova AO. et al. 5-MeO-DMT review. J Psychopharmacol 2022"])
X("mescaline-peyote","Mescaline (peyote, San Pedro)","psy","Phenethylamine psychedelic from cacti.","Peyote (Lophophora williamsii), San Pedro (Echinopsis pachanoi), Peruvian torch; synthetic mescaline.",
"Peyote has been used in Mexico and the American Southwest for at least 5,000 years. Arthur Heffter isolated mescaline in 1897 and Ernst Späth synthesized it in 1919. Aldous Huxley's 'The Doors of Perception' (1954) described his experience. The Native American Church's peyote use is federally protected.",
"No approved use.","Used ceremonially and recreationally; peyote is slow-growing and threatened.","Mescaline and peyote are US Schedule I, with an exemption for Native American Church use; San Pedro cacti are legal to grow as ornamentals in many places. UN 1971 Schedule I (mescaline).",
["Mescaline is long and gentle compared with LSD: warm, colorful, and grounded, often with deep empathy and a strong connection to nature.","Nausea and vomiting are very common at the start.","Physical risks are low but include raised heart rate and blood pressure."],
["Effects begin 45 to 90 minutes after eating, often with nausea.","Colors and emotions intensify.","The peak at about 3 to 5 hours.","Long plateau.","Taper over many hours.","Insomnia.","Next-day tiredness."],
{"oral":t("45-90 min","1-2 h","3-5 h","5-8 h","8-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Cactus eaten or brewed; synthetic capsules."},"Physical toxicity is low; panic and accidents are the main risks.","Peyote buttons are small, round, spineless, gray-green; San Pedro is a tall columnar cactus.","Mescaline is a phenethylamine, related to the 2C drugs.",
ext=("Mescaline is absorbed slowly from the gut and needs much more than LSD to act, because it binds 5-HT2A weakly.","Nausea and vomiting occur early.","At peak, 5-HT2A activation produces vivid colors and pattern visuals.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise modestly.","Half-life about 6 hours.","Afterglow and fatigue."),src=S+["Huxley A. The Doors of Perception (1954)","El-Seedi HR. et al. Prehistoric peyote use. J Ethnopharmacol 2005"])
X("2c-b","2C-B","psy","Synthetic phenethylamine psychedelic.","Nexus, Venus, bees, tusi (in Latin America, 'tusi' often contains ketamine and MDMA instead).",
"Alexander Shulgin synthesized 2C-B in 1974 and described it in PiHKAL (1991). It was sold as 'Nexus' in Germany in the 1980s, then banned.","None; briefly used in German psychotherapy.","Popular in club settings.","US Schedule I (1995); UN 1971 Schedule II (2001).",
["2C-B combines mild empathogenic warmth with colorful visuals; it is shorter and more 'body' than LSD.","Stronger levels bring intense visuals and can be overwhelming.","'Tusi' or 'pink cocaine' sold in clubs usually contains ketamine, MDMA and other drugs, not 2C-B."],
["Effects begin 30 to 75 minutes after swallowing.","Body tingles and colors brighten.","Peak at 1.5 to 3 hours.","Plateau 1 to 2 hours.","Taper over 2 hours.","Light afterglow.","Usually little next-day effect."],
{"oral":t("30-75 min","1-1.5 h","1.5-3 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-8 h","next day")},{"oral":"Pills or powder."},"Physical risks include raised heart rate and blood pressure.","Powder or small pills."+CW,"2C-B is a partial 5-HT2A agonist.",
ext=("2C-B is absorbed from the gut and acts as a partial agonist at 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C receptors.","Heart rate rises modestly; nausea is possible.","At peak, visual and tactile effects are strong; high levels cause anxiety and confusion.","Mixing with stimulants raises heart risk.","Half-life is about 2.5 hours.","Little hangover for most."),src=S+["Shulgin A, Shulgin A. PiHKAL (1991)"])
X("2c-x","Other 2C drugs (2C-I, 2C-E, 2C-T-7, 2C-P and others)","psy","Synthetic phenethylamine psychedelics.","2C-I, 2C-E, 2C-T-2, 2C-T-7 (blue mystic), 2C-P, 2C-C, 2C-D.",
"Most were described by Shulgin in PiHKAL. Several were sold online in the 2000s; 2C-T-7 was linked to deaths and scheduled in 2002, and others in 2012.","None.","Sold as research chemicals or as LSD or ecstasy.","Most are US Schedule I.",
["2C drugs differ a lot: 2C-E is intense and long, 2C-I more stimulating, 2C-P very long and strong, 2C-T-7 linked to deaths when snorted or mixed.","Many cause body discomfort, nausea and strong visuals.","Potency differences mean an amount that is mild with one can be overwhelming with another."],
["Onset in 30 to 90 minutes.","Visuals and body load build.","Peak 2 to 4 hours.","Long plateau.","Taper over hours.","Insomnia.","Next-day fatigue."],
{"oral":t("30-90 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","3-6 h","6-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Pills or powder."},"Deaths have been linked to 2C-T-7 and 2C-T-21, often with other drugs.","Powders or pills."+CW,"Each binds 5-HT2A with different potency.",
ext=("Absorption and onset vary by compound; 2C-P and 2C-E are slow.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise.","At peak, strong 5-HT2A effects; seizures and overheating are reported with some compounds.","2C-T compounds inhibit MAO weakly, raising interaction risk.","Durations vary widely, up to many hours.","Fatigue and insomnia follow."),src=S+["Shulgin A, Shulgin A. PiHKAL (1991)"])
X("nbome","NBOMe drugs (25I-NBOMe, 25C-NBOMe, 25B-NBOMe)","psy","Extremely potent synthetic phenethylamines.","N-bomb, smiles, 25I; often sold as LSD.",
"Created by Ralf Heim in 2003 and studied by David Nichols' lab as research tools. Sold from about 2010 on blotters as 'legal acid'; linked to many deaths and controlled in 2013.","None.","Usually taken by people who think they have LSD.","US Schedule I (2013); UN 1971 Schedule I (2015).",
["NBOMes produce LSD-like visuals but with much more body stress: racing heart, vasoconstriction, agitation.","They are active at tiny amounts, so a blotter can contain a toxic quantity; unlike LSD, they can kill.","A bitter or numbing taste on blotter is a warning sign: 'If it's bitter, it's a spitter.'"],
["Effects begin 15 to 60 minutes after taking.","Visuals and stimulation.","Peak at 2 to 4 hours.","Plateau several hours.","Taper over hours.","Exhaustion.","Next-day fatigue."],
{"sublingual-buccal":t("15-60 min","1 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-10 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"sublingual-buccal":"Blotter held in the mouth."},
"NBOMes cause seizures, overheating, heart attacks, kidney failure and death.","Usually blotter paper identical to LSD; a bitter or numbing taste suggests an NBOMe."+CW,"NBOMes are full 5-HT2A agonists and also constrict blood vessels.",
ext=("NBOMes are destroyed by first-pass metabolism if swallowed, so blotters are held in the mouth.","Vasoconstriction, fast heart rate and high blood pressure appear early.","At peak, seizures, overheating, agitation and arrhythmias can occur; some deaths involved violent trauma.","Rhabdomyolysis and kidney failure follow severe cases.","Effects last several hours.","Survivors of severe poisoning may have lasting kidney or brain injury."),src=S+["Suzuki J. et al. 25I-NBOMe toxicity. Psychosomatics 2015"])
X("dox","DOx amphetamines (DOM, DOB, DOC, DOI)","psy","Long-acting psychedelic amphetamines.","DOM (STP), DOB, DOC, DOI.",
"DOM was described by Shulgin in 1963 and appeared as 'STP' in Haight-Ashbury in 1967, where many people were hospitalized because it was much longer and stronger than expected.","None.","Sometimes sold as LSD on blotter.","DOM, DOB: US Schedule I, UN Schedule I; DOC: controlled in many countries.",
["DOx drugs are very long, often lasting most of a day, with LSD-like visuals and strong stimulation.","Slow onset tempts people to take more.","Vasoconstriction can be severe; DOB has caused deaths."],
["Onset 1 to 3 hours.","Slow build.","Peak 4 to 8 hours.","Long plateau.","Taper over many hours.","Insomnia.","Effects may linger into the next day."],
{"oral":t("1-3 h","2-4 h","4-8 h","8-14 h","14-24 h","24-36 h","next 1-2 days")},{"oral":"Blotter or pills."},"Severe vasoconstriction, seizures and overheating are reported.","Usually blotter."+CW,"DOx compounds are potent 5-HT2A agonists with long half-lives.",
ext=("DOx compounds are absorbed slowly, with onset often taking hours.","Blood vessels constrict and heart rate rises.","At peak, intense psychedelic effects with stimulation; vasospasm can cause limb ischemia.","Very long duration increases exhaustion and risk.","Effects can last a day or more.","Sleep loss and anxiety may persist."),src=S+["Shulgin A, Shulgin A. PiHKAL (1991)"])
X("ibogaine","Ibogaine (iboga)","psy","Indole alkaloid from the Tabernanthe iboga shrub; oneirogen.","Iboga root bark; ibogaine hydrochloride.",
"Used in the Bwiti religion of Gabon and Cameroon. Isolated in 1901. Sold in France as Lambarène until 1966. Howard Lotsof reported in 1962 that it ended his heroin cravings; clinics in Mexico and elsewhere offer it for addiction. Texas funded ibogaine research in 2025.",
"No approved use; studied for opioid use disorder and traumatic brain injury.","Mostly sought for addiction treatment, not recreation.","US Schedule I; legal or unregulated in Mexico, New Zealand (prescription), and others.",
["Ibogaine produces a long, dreamlike state with life-review visions, followed by many hours of wakeful reflection.","It is physically punishing: severe nausea, loss of coordination, and inability to walk.","It prolongs the QT interval of the heart and has caused sudden cardiac deaths, especially in people with heart problems, low potassium or magnesium, or opioids in their system."],
["Effects begin 1 to 3 hours after swallowing.","Nausea and ataxia.","Visionary phase for 4 to 8 hours.","Reflective phase for many hours.","Residual effects for a day or more.","Exhaustion.","Fatigue for days."],
{"oral":t("1-3 h","2-4 h","4-8 h","8-20 h","20-36 h","1-3 days","days")},{"oral":"Capsules or root bark."},"Ibogaine causes dangerous heart rhythms and has caused many deaths.","Root bark or capsules of uncertain purity.","Ibogaine is metabolized by CYP2D6 to noribogaine, which lasts for days.",
ext=("Ibogaine is absorbed slowly and converted by CYP2D6 into noribogaine.","Nausea, vomiting and severe loss of coordination occur early.","At peak, ibogaine blocks cardiac hERG potassium channels, lengthening the QT interval and risking torsades de pointes and sudden death.","Medical screening (ECG, electrolytes) and monitoring are essential; deaths have occurred at unregulated clinics.","Noribogaine persists for days, extending heart risk.","People with opioid tolerance can overdose if they resume opioids afterward, because tolerance drops."),src=S+["Koenig X, Hilber K. The anti-addiction drug ibogaine and the heart. Molecules 2015","Cherian KN. et al. Nature Medicine 2024"])
X("rc-tryptamines","Research-chemical tryptamines (4-AcO-DMT, 4-HO-MET, AMT, 5-MeO-MiPT, DPT)","psy","Synthetic tryptamines related to psilocybin and DMT.","4-AcO-DMT (psilacetin), 4-HO-MET, AMT, 5-MeO-MiPT (moxy), DPT, 5-MeO-DALT.",
"Many were described by Shulgin in TiHKAL (1997) and sold online from the 2000s.","None.","Sold online as 'legal mushrooms' and research chemicals.","AMT is US Schedule I; others vary or fall under analog laws.",
["4-AcO-DMT feels much like psilocybin; 4-HO-MET is more visual and lighter emotionally.","AMT is long and stimulating and also inhibits MAO, raising interaction risk; 5-MeO-MiPT is tactile and less visual.","Deaths have been linked to AMT and 5-MeO tryptamines combined with other drugs."],
["Onset varies from 20 minutes to 3 hours by drug.","Body load and visuals build.","Peaks range from 2 to 6 hours.","Plateau varies.","Taper varies.","Fatigue.","AMT may last into next day."],
{"oral":t("20 min-3 h","1-2 h","2-6 h","2-8 h","4-16 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Capsules or powder."},"Risks include overheating, serotonin syndrome (especially AMT with other drugs), and accidents.","Powders or capsules."+CW,"These act on 5-HT2A; AMT also releases monoamines and inhibits MAO.",
ext=("Absorption varies; 4-AcO-DMT converts to psilocin.","Nausea is common.","At peak, psychedelic effects; AMT adds stimulation.","AMT's MAO inhibition can cause serotonin syndrome with other drugs.","Durations range from a few hours to over a day.","Insomnia and fatigue follow."),src=S+["Shulgin A, Shulgin A. TiHKAL (1997)"])
